<!--
  Vendored copy — provenance
  ==========================
  Source:      modelica/fmi-ls-ref, pull request #36
               (https://github.com/modelica/fmi-ls-ref/pull/36)
  Retrieved:   2026-07-20 — cloned repo, fetched PR #36 head (commit 12e0990), confirmed via
               `git show` that the schema content is unchanged since commit 566241e ("Match
               SRMD attributes, drop top-level attribute"); the PR's later commit only touches
               an example/documentation file, not this schema.
  Status:      PR #36 is NOT YET MERGED upstream as of retrieval. Vendored and wired into
               P515_SCHEMA.xsd on the explicit instruction to "execute the P515 plan assuming
               .../pull/36 is merged" (2026-07-20). If the PR changes materially before merging,
               this file and the P515 schema/Schematron built on it need to be re-checked against
               the final merged version.
  Modified from upstream: (1) the two dependency schemaLocation values below were changed from
               the PR's raw.githubusercontent.com URLs to local relative filenames
               (fmi3LayeredStandardManifest.xsd, fmi3Annotation.xsd), matching this project's
               established convention of vendoring same-directory dependencies (see SRMD.xsd's
               relative import of STC.xsd) instead of resolving imports over the network at
               validation time. (2) The fmi3Annotation.xsd dependency was changed from
               <xs:import> to <xs:include> (2026-07-20, discovered during validation, not a
               judgment call to flag lightly): this schema document has no targetNamespace of
               its own, and fmi3Annotation.xsd also has no targetNamespace — importing "no
               namespace" components into a schema that is itself in "no namespace" is invalid
               under the XSD 1.0 import constraints (confirmed via lxml/libxml2 schema
               compilation, which rejected the original <xs:import
               schemaLocation="fmi3Annotation.xsd"/> form with: "the attribute 'namespace' must
               be existent if the importing schema has no target namespace"); <xs:include> is
               the correct construct for pulling in same-namespace (here, no-namespace)
               components. This defect appears to exist in the actual upstream PR #36 content
               as retrieved (it used the same <xs:import>, just with a raw.githubusercontent.com
               URL) — flagged to the user as a real deviation from the literal PR text, not a
               silent fix. (3) The @checksumType attribute on TRelated originally declared BOTH
               type="xs:string" AND an inline <xs:simpleType> child restricting to the SHA3-*/
               SHA-* enumeration — also invalid under XSD 1.0 (an attribute's `type` and inline
               `<simpleType>` child are mutually exclusive), also confirmed via lxml/libxml2
               compilation, also present in the upstream PR #36 content as retrieved. Fixed by
               removing the redundant type="xs:string" and keeping the inline simpleType
               restriction (no loss of constraint, just removal of the conflicting duplicate
               declaration). Two genuine schema defects found in PR #36 as currently written,
               both flagged to the user rather than silently absorbed.
  Note on optionality: in this upstream schema, @checksum and @checksumType on TRelated are both
               OPTIONAL (checksumType merely carries a default of SHA3-256; checksum has no
               default and no use="required"). LOTAR requires checksums for archival (PD1), so
               (2026-09-05, D-23: now P520_SCHEMA.xsd / P520_Related_Type / lotar-p520-archival.sch.)
               P515_SCHEMA.xsd defines P515_Related_Type (xs:extension of TRelated) and a
               companion Schematron layer (lotar-p515-archival.sch) enforces @checksum as
               mandatory-for-archival on top of this loose base type — the same XSD-stays-
               spec-faithful / Schematron-enforces-mandatory pattern used throughout the FMI2/
               FMI3 archival profiles, since XSD restriction cannot tighten an inherited
               attribute's `use` from optional to required within a single derivation step.
-->
